	Shared memory is not copied, (unlike fork's copy-on-write)
	All attached process see the same physical memory 


Key & ID 

	* key is used to identify the shared memory segment 
	* Kernel converts the key into a shared memory ID (shmid)
	* Processes use shmid to attach the segment 

shmat() Explanation
	
	void *shmat(int shmid, const void *shmaddr, int shmflg);

		1. shmid 

			shared memory ID returned shmget()

		2. shmaddr 

			usually set to null or 0 
				
				user doesn't know the virtual address where shared memory should be placed.
				Shared memory is created in kernel space, not user space 

			if shmaddr == NULL 

				system automatically chooses a suitable address

		3. shmflag 

			flags (eg : read-only)

Summary 

	* Shared memory is created in kernel space 
	* It is attached in user space 
	* Second parameter of shmat() is always 0 / NULL in most cases 
	* if shmaddr is NULL, the system selects the address automatically 
	* Shared memory allow fast IPC since no copying involved 














	









































